't discard B4. \n\nThe F2s are a pair, so don't discard them. \n\nW1 is two, so discard one. \n\nSo possible options: Play W1, Play W6, Play T5, Play W7, Play W8. \n\nIf the user discards W1, they still have another W1. That's safe. \n\nBut maybe the user is trying to form a concealed hand. If they discard a tile that's part of a meld, it might not be concealed. But since the user already has a Peng of B2 (exposed), the rest are concealed. \n\nSo the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential concealed meld. \n\nBetween W1, W6, T5, etc. \n\nIf the user discards W1, they still have another. \n\nIf they discard W6, they lose that tile. \n\nConsidering that the B3-B4-B5 is a potential chow, keeping B4 is important. \n\nSo the best choice is to discard W6, T5, or W1. \n\nLooking at the unshown counts, W1 has 3 left, so discarding W1 is safe. \n\nBut the user has two W1s. Discarding one W1 would leave one in hand. \n\nAlternatively, if the user is aiming for a pair of F2, keeping F2s is better. \n\nSo the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. \n\nBetween W6, T5, W7, W8. \n\nIf the user discards W6, they can still form other melds. \n\nI think the best move here is to discard W6. Because the other tiles like B4 are important for potential chows. \n\nSo the answer is Play W6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has a Peng of B2 and needs three more melds and a pair. The B3 and B4 can form a chow with B5 if drawn. Discarding W6 is safe as it doesn't block potential melds and there are still two W6s left in the wall.  \nReason: Discarding W6 maintains the possibility of forming the B3-B4-B5 chow and avoids losing a tile that doesn't contribute to the current hand's structure.  \nAnswer: Play W6"}
